  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nEarly this morning, the winter storm system is underway over the \neastern CWA. Light snow is expected to move southwest through the \narea through the morning hours today. This area of snow appears tied \nto a 700mb vort max moving counter-clockwise around the north side \nof the mid-level low currently centered over the central MO/AR \nborder. Temperatures early this morning have remained right around \nfreezing and as such, snow has struggled to stick to anything but \nelevated surfaces. With warm surface temperatures, ground \ntemperatures, and light snowfall rates, it my be difficult for snow \nto accumulate on sidewalks, and roadways unless rates can overcome \nwarm ground and air temps. However snow will accumulate on grassy \nsurfaces as well as elevated surface like bridges and overpasses. \nAll told, by the time the storm system exits by late this \nmorning/early afternoon, 1-2 inches are forecast for areas east of \nHighway 65. From Highway 65 back to western Missouri only a dusting \nto less than an inch is expected, with only flurries expected across \nthe KC Metro. As the system exits this afternoon highs are expected \nto rise into the upper 30s to lower 40s fostering additional \nmelting. A quick moving weak surface ridge of high pressure is \nexpected to move through the area tonight aiding in lows dropping \nback into the low to mid 20s. This surface ridge will slide south \nand east of the area tomorrow with WAA getting underway as the upper \nlevel flow become quasi-zonal. This will allow highs to mid 40s to \nmid 50s. Tomorrow night a upper level shortwave trough with dig \nthrough the northern Plains into the Upper Midwest. This will force \nmore of a Pacific front through the CWA and downslope westerly in \nthe wake of the system will aid in highs rising into the 50s on \nSaturday. \n\nSaturday night a upper level trough will move from the eastern \nRockies into the Plains forcing a cold front into the area and \nbringing showers (70-90%) to the CWA. Showers will exit the area by \nSunday afternoon however, CAA behind the front will drop highs into \nthe 40s to near 50. Monday, upper level flow becomes zonal across \nthe region with late day WAA getting underway which will push highs \ninto the mid 40s to mid 50s. Zonal upper level flow will continue on \nTuesday with continued WAA raising highs into the 50s. Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday, a upper level trough will dig into the Four Corners \nregion allow lee cyclogenesis to deepen. This will tighten the \npressure gradient across the area with strong WAA expected. That \nwill push highs up into the mid 50s to mid 60s on Wednesday.",
